<bold>0.0314</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">37.08</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.7&#x02013;45.8</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sweat chloride (mmol&#x000b7;L<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">94.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">93.2&#x02013;99.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.8717</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">97</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">82.0&#x02013;110.7</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">%BAL neutrophils</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">74.6</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">47.9&#x02013;84.4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.21&#x000d7;10<sup>&#x02212;5</sup></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.7&#x02013;31.3</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0843</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">22.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10.0&#x02013;38.7</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">%BAL macrophages</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.4&#x02013;50.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.00&#x000d7;10<sup>&#x02212;4</sup></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">72.4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50.0&#x02013;83.0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.8763</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">70</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">54.7&#x02013;82.7</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">%BAL lymphocytes</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.1&#x02013;5.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.1401</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9.3</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.3&#x02013;15.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>0.0048</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.85</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.0&#x02013;7.0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">%BAL eosinophils</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0&#x02013;1.0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.2573</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0&#x02013;0.0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.5242</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0&#x02013;1.0</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Demographic and clinical data are summarised for 12 visits from 11 PE patients and 17 visits from 17 DC patients, along with comparisons with the stable cystic fibrosis (CF) cross-sectional cohort. Categorical variables were analysed using chi-square tests, while continuous variables were assessed using the Mann&#x02013;Whitney U-test. Reported p-values reflect differences between the PE and DC cohorts compared with the CF cross-sectional cohort. Fixed patient characteristics, such as sex, F508del status, sweat chloride levels and pancreatic insufficiency, were counted per unique subject. Variable characteristics, including modulator status, bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) culture results, antibiotic usage, age, %BAL cell counts and gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) diagnosis, were counted per visit. Five PE patients were receiving prophylactic antibiotic treatment at the time of their visit. Modulator use refers to lumacaftor&#x02013;ivacaftor. GORD diagnosis data are relevant only for the DC cohort. Sweat chloride levels, pancreatic insufficiency, antibiotic usage, genotype and modulator use information were not available for the DC cohort.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>At ages 0&#x02013;2&#x02005;years, NE activity, MPO activity, IL-8 and %PMN were not able to predict future bronchiectasis (<xref rid="F5" ref-type="fig">figure 5a&#x02013;d</xref>). However, MetO, %OxMet, NAcMet and ornithine were sensitive predictors (p&#x0003c;0.05, AUC&#x0003e;0.7; <xref rid="F5" ref-type="fig">figure 5e&#x02013;h</xref>; further details in <ext-link xlink:href="https://publications.ersnet.org/lookup/doi/10.1183/23120541.00238-2025#supplementary" ext-link-type="uri">supplementary table S7</ext-link>). Combining MetO and ornithine improved sensitivity (AUC=0.79, p=0.0123; <xref rid="F5" ref-type="fig">figure 5i</xref>). Within the same cohort at ages 3&#x02013;6&#x02005;years, MetO, %OxMet, ornithine and NAcMet remained sensitive predictors of future bronchiectasis (p&#x0003c;0.05; <xref rid="F5" ref-type="fig">figure 5n&#x02013;q</xref>), but conventional biomarkers (%PMN, IL-8, MPO and NE) were also sensitive predictors of bronchiectasis at age 9 years. Cut-off threshold values for each tested variable are given in <ext-link xlink:href="https://publications.ersnet.org/lookup/doi/10.1183/23120541.00238-2025#supplementary" ext-link-type="uri">supplementary table S8</ext-link>. Except arginine, all metabolites tested predicted bronchiectasis development if assessed at 3&#x02013;6&#x02005;years (<ext-link xlink:href="https://publications.ersnet.org/lookup/doi/10.1183/23120541.00238-2025#supplementary" ext-link-type="uri">supplementary figure S6</ext-link>). Finally, within ages 3&#x02013;6&#x02005;years, the combination of MPO and NE activities was the best predictor of future bronchiectasis (<xref rid="F5" ref-type="fig">figure 5r</xref>).</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="s5"><title>Discussion</title><p>In the current study, we identified 10 metabolites in early CF BAL fluid robustly associated with SLD and neutrophilic inflammation: arginine, eicosadienoic acid, lysine, methionine, MetO, NAcMet, ornithine, phenylalanine, threonine and TML. These metabolites were selected by consensus with models predicting chest CT-scored SLD, BAL neutrophils, and BAL fluid activities of MPO and NE, and thus reflect robust relationships with pathological neutrophil activity. We followed up by analysing BAL of a separate cohort of well-matched children who either did or did not exhibit bronchiectasis at age 9 years, and found that MetO, NAcMet and ornithine measured at ages 0&#x02013;2&#x02005;years sensitively predicted the age 9 bronchiectasis outcome. Bronchiectasis at age 9 years is expected to be less reversible than that occurring earlier in life, and lack of bronchiectasis at this age in CF indicates preserved lung health. The combination of MetO and ornithine was more sensitive than either alone. By contrast, NE activity, %PMN and IL-8 were poor predictors when measured at ages 0&#x02013;2&#x02005;years, although they were sensitive at ages 3&#x02013;6&#x02005;years, as were all other tested metabolites except arginine.</p><p>A previous study by S<sc>ly</sc>
<italic toggle="yes">et al.</italic> demonstrated that detectible BAL NE activity was the most sensitive risk factor for bronchiectasis development from 3&#x02005;months to 3&#x02005;years of age, and the most sensitive risk factor for bronchiectasis persistence from 3&#x02005;months to 1 or 3&#x02005;years of age [<xref rid="C23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>]. Thus, it may seem surprising that in the current study we found NE activity at ages 0&#x02013;2 years to be a poor predictor of bronchiectasis at age 9 years. Important differences in our study include its outcome (future bronchiectasis was assessed much later in life), the analysis of NE as a continuous rather than a binary variable, the use of ROC to evaluate biomarker potential, and a smaller sample size. Interestingly, H<sc>orati</sc>
<italic toggle="yes">et al.</italic> also found that NE activity was not a sensitive predictor of bronchiectasis progression in toddlers with CF [<xref rid="C24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]. Most importantly, the results of the current study have identified that specific metabolites related to inflammatory processes &#x02013; MetO (and ratio %OxMet), NAcMet and ornithine &#x02013; may at certain periods of development be even better predictors of bronchiectasis than the effector proteins associated with them.</p><p>This study highlights the strong potential of BAL fluid metabolites for tracking and predicting future bronchiectasis in very young people with CF. Notably, MetO and %OxMet were the best single variable predictors of future bronchiectasis. MetO and %OxMet have received considerable attention in recent years as potential early CF disease biomarkers, arising predominantly from MPO-derived hypochlorous acid (HOCl), which has the potential to cause irreversible macromolecular damage [<xref rid="C20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="C25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="C29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>]. %OxMet may have increased clinical robustness considering its resilience against variable dilution of BAL. Considering the overall high methionine abundance in CF airways, free methionine is likely to be a major source of MetO, but it may also arise in proteins and be liberated by proteolysis [<xref rid="C30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>, <xref rid="C31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>]. NAcMet, another predictor of bronchiectasis, is anticipated to arise from protease activity owing to high <italic toggle="yes">N</italic>-terminal methionine acetylation [<xref rid="C32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>, <xref rid="C33" ref-type="bibr">33</xref>]. Ornithine, another predictor of bronchiectasis, is a product of arginase activity, which decreases nitric oxide by limiting nitric oxide synthase activity, and also increases polyamines, including spermine, which can limit bronchial relaxation [<xref rid="C34" ref-type="bibr">34</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="C38" ref-type="bibr">38</xref>]. Ultimately, combining MetO and ornithine yielded the best prediction of bronchiectasis, perhaps because this combination captures two independent axes of airway metabolic disturbance: (chlorinating) oxidative stress and disrupted arginine metabolism.</p><p>Many observations in this study align with the metabolic activities of granule-releasing, immunomodulatory and metabolically active (GRIM) neutrophils in early CF [<xref rid="C7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. These include the multiple metabolite products of MPO, NE and ARG1 activities noted above, as GRIM neutrophils actively exocytose these molecules into the lumen [<xref rid="C39" ref-type="bibr">39</xref>]. Interestingly, BAL fluid adenosine diphosphate (ADP) was associated with %Mac, and inversely with %PMN, suggesting that it may reflect relative airway macrophage rather than neutrophil relative content and function [<xref rid="C40" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>].</p><p>Our data suggest that the transition from infancy to early childhood sees a metabolic shift in the CF lungs as disease becomes established. This predominantly sees metabolites associated with neutrophil-driven pathology increasing over time, as in the cases of MetO and ornithine, although NAcMet decreases (perhaps owing to diminished anti-proteolytic <italic toggle="yes">N</italic>-acetylation). Because such pathology is associated with SLD manifestations that are difficult to resolve, the utility of sensitive, early-stage biomarkers to predict neutrophil-driven lung damage could be high. In the era of increasing use of highly effective CFTR modulators, the metabolites identified in this study may aid clinicians in judging the effectiveness of and need for modulators and additional interventions. Although not yet available, several are in early trials [<xref rid="C41" ref-type="bibr">41</xref>].</p><sec id="s5a"><title>Limitations</title><p>One key limitation of BAL biomarker studies is the invasive nature of BAL collection, especially in the light of alternatives such as induced sputum (IS) and exhaled breath condensate (EBC). However, BAL fluid metabolites have consistently proven to be the most sensitive and reliable indicators of disease progression, particularly in children with CF in whom neutrophil-driven lung disease is not yet fully established [<xref rid="C42" ref-type="bibr">42</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="C46" ref-type="bibr">46</xref>]. Future studies could determine whether these biomarkers function as well in IS or EBC. If not, while longitudinal BAL remains invasive and impractical, our study suggests that a surveillance bronchoscopy in the first years of life could offer important clinical insight into early inflammation and disease risk. Our study also suggests that multilobe sampling may be unnecessary, considering that RML and lingula BAL metabolites were highly correlated.</p><p>Another important limitation is that most of the people with CF in our study were CFTR modulator na&#x000ef;ve at the time of sample collection, and those using modulators were using lumacaftor&#x02013;ivacaftor, which is no longer the first-line drug in class. Future studies should attempt to replicate the current findings in people of a similar age with CF using state-of-the-art modulators. Yet another cohort limitation was a lack of healthy control data for comparison, as BAL fluid from healthy children is not readily available for research. The DC cohort in the current study represents various levels of CFTR-independent airway pathologies, not a disease-free baseline.</p><p>Finally, our metabolomics technique under-reports hydrophobic molecules and polyamines owing to issues of poor or extreme chromatographic retention, respectively [<xref rid="C47" ref-type="bibr">47</xref>, <xref rid="C48" ref-type="bibr">48</xref>]. This limited our ability to analyse such molecules in the current study.</p></sec><sec id="s5b"><title>Conclusions and future directions</title><p>We identified BAL metabolites that tracked early CF SLD and neutrophilic inflammation in a cross-sectional cohort of young children across two CF centres. A subset of these metabolites (MetO, ornithine, NAcMet and the ratio %OxMet) sensitively predicted bronchiectasis in an independent longitudinal cohort of CF infants and toddlers. These metabolites outperformed conventional biomarkers, including NE activity. Further studies are needed to validate these metabolites as prognostic tools and to explore their translation into less invasive specimens. Future research should also focus on developing targeted anti-inflammatory strategies based on the pathways from which these biomarkers are hypothesised to originate, namely HOCl production by MPO, NE proteolysis and ARG1 activity.</p></sec></sec></body><back><ack><title>Acknowledgements</title><p>We would like to acknowledge all children and families who participated in the clinical studies, Merlijn Bonte for performing PRAGMA-CF scoring of chest CT scans, and all paediatric pulmonologists at all sites for performing bronchoscopies and collecting samples.</p><p>Finally, we acknowledge the following sources of visual media used in our graphical abstract: BioRender (cell and human images), ChemDraw 23.1.1 (chemical structures), Protein Databank (MPO and NE 3D structures; rcsb.org) and R<sc>osenow</sc>
<italic toggle="yes">et al.</italic> [<xref rid="C15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>] (PRAGMA-CF-scored CT cross-section example).</p></ack><fn-group><fn><p>Data associated with this study are included with the <ext-link xlink:href="https://publications.ersnet.org/lookup/doi/10.1183/23120541.00238-2025#supplementary" ext-link-type="uri">supplementary material</ext-link>.</p></fn><fn><p>Provenance: Submitted article, peer reviewed.</p></fn><fn><p>Ethics approval: Human subjects were consented and underwent bronchoscopy, providing BAL fluid, at multiple sites with multiple IRB review.</p></fn><fn fn-type="COI-statement"><p>Conflict of interest: S.M. Stephen Stick reports support for the present manuscript from National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC).</p><p>Conflict of interest: C.R. Esther reports support for the present manuscript from ESTHER24R0, ESTHER22Y2-SVC, Synergy; and consultancy fees from Cystic Fibrosis Foundation.</p><p>Conflict of interest: H.M. Janssens reports support for the present manuscript from National Institute of Health (NIH) (R01 HL126603), Cystic Fibrosis Foundation (TIROUV19A0), Dutch CF Foundation (NCFS-HITCF), Sophia Foundation (SSWO19-21); grants from Vertex Innovation Award (VIA); payment or honoraria for lectures, presentations, speakers&#x02019; bureaux, manuscript writing or educational events for Vertex Pharmaceuticals; and a leadership role with ECFS-CTN.</p><p>Conflict of interest: J.T. Lyles reports stock or stock options with LiIfeMine Therapeutics.</p><p>Conflict of interest: L. Guglani reports support for the present manuscript from Hertz Family Foundation Grant.</p><p>Conflict of interest: L.J.M. Slimmen reports support for the present manuscript from Cystic Fibrosis Foundation.</p><p>Conflict of interest: J.D. Chandler reports support for the present manuscript from National Institutes of Health (NIH) (R01 HL150658), Australian National Health and Medical Research Council (APP1183640); grants from NIH and Cystic Fibros